# ICPay — Backend ↔ Frontend compliance map

Backend endpoints that exist on the canister but are NOT implemented in the
frontend. Status checked: 2026-08-08.

## Quick numbers

- Backend public methods: **43**
- Wired on the frontend (has a WalletActor call site): **24**
- Missing on the frontend: **19**

No frontend-declared method is missing from the backend: every method in the
`WalletActor` interface is backed by a real canister method. All gaps are
"backend present, frontend absent", never the reverse.

---

## 1. Declared in the WalletActor type, but never called

These have both a backend endpoint and a frontend interface entry
(`services/wallet.ts`), but no call site anywhere. Wiring is what's missing.

| Method | Backend module | Suggested frontend home |
|---|---|---|
| `health` | `api/v1/Health.mo` | `services/client.ts` (query) |
| `register` | `api/v1/Auth.mo` | `services/auth/auth.ts` — first-time handle claim on login |
| `getUsernameTreasury` | `api/v1/UsernameSale.mo` | `services/buy/buy.ts` — username purchase page |
| `syncDeposits` | `api/v1/Deposit.mo` | `services/deposit/deposit.ts` — deposit refresh action |
| `getTransactionDetail` | `api/v1/Transactions.mo` | `services/transactions/transactions.ts` — tx detail view |
| `getSettings` | `api/v1/Settings.mo` | new `services/settings/settings.ts` (query) |
| `updateSettings` | `api/v1/Settings.mo` | new `services/settings/settings.ts` (call) |

Note: `getSettings` / `updateSettings` are declared in the actor type yet no
settings feature exists on the frontend at all.

---

## 2. Not in the WalletActor type at all

No interface declaration, no caller. These are backend-only. Nearly all are
operator/admin-gated (via `MiddlewareAuth.effectiveCaller`), not user-facing UI.

| Method | Backend module | Nature |
|---|---|---|
| `reserveUsername` | `api/v1/Admin.mo` | Admin — reserved-handle lifecycle |
| `releaseUsername` | `api/v1/Admin.mo` | Admin — reserved-handle lifecycle |
| `listReservedUsernames` | `api/v1/Admin.mo` | Admin — reserved-handle lifecycle |
| `refreshLedgers` | `api/v1/Ledgers.mo` | Operator — ledger registry refresh |
| `uploadTokenWasmChunk` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — launch wasm staging |
| `sealTokenWasm` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — launch wasm staging |
| `resetTokenWasm` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — launch wasm staging |
| `sweepTokenRevenue` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — revenue maintenance |
| `topUpToken` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — canister top-up |
| `reserveTokenSymbol` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — launch bookkeeping |
| `releaseFailedCanister` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — cleanup |
| `registerLaunchedLedgers` | `api/v1/Token.mo` | Operator — launch bookkeeping |

These should be surfaced through the operations CLI / an admin surface, not the
end-user screens.

---

## 3. The user-facing gaps (what actually matters)

The five that belong in the product UI:

1. **`register`** — first-time handle claim.
   `openBackendSession()` (`services/auth/auth.ts`) calls only `actor.login()`,
   which creates the user record with `username = null`. There is no step to
   claim a username on first login.
2. **`getSettings` / `updateSettings`** — the settings screen does not exist.
3. **`getTransactionDetail`** — transaction detail view not implemented.
4. **`syncDeposits`** — deposit list has no manual refresh action.
5. **`getUsernameTreasury`** — username purchase page does not display the
   treasury principal.

---

## 4. Frontend semantic disconnect — username is permanent

The backend treats the username as an immutable, set-once identity:

- `AuthService.register` → rejects with `"Username is permanent and cannot be
  changed"` once set.
- `UserService.updateUsername` → identical rule (same validator, same reserve
  + taken checks, same `setUsername`).

So `register` and `updateUsername` are functionally the same one-shot claim.
The frontend currently:

- never calls `register` at first login, and
- exposes `updateUsername` as a changeable profile field, which contradicts the
  backend's permanent-username model.

Until this is resolved, a first-time user has no onboarding path to claim a
handle, and a claimed handle cannot be re-claimed or edited as the UI implies.

---

